1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,983
Access needs a neighborhood. Packages are those neighborhoods.

2
00:00:04,983 --> 00:00:08,359
A package is a namespace — and a boundary.

3
00:00:08,359 --> 00:00:12,860
It prevents name collisions and shapes who collaborates.

4
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:17,682
On disk and at runtime, package plus class name is identity.

5
00:00:17,682 --> 00:00:21,861
Treat package structure as architecture you can see.

6
00:00:21,861 --> 00:00:26,362
Folders tell the truth about ownership — or they should.

7
00:00:26,362 --> 00:00:27,866
Episode Twelve.

8
00:00:27,866 --> 00:00:32,780
Packages — namespaces, boundaries, and ownership.

9
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:35,451
package com.acme.orders.domain;

10
00:00:35,451 --> 00:00:40,621
That line is not decoration — it is part of the binary name.

11
00:00:40,621 --> 00:00:46,136
com.acme.OrderService is not the same as com.other.OrderService.

12
00:00:46,136 --> 00:00:51,651
Folders should match the package declaration. Java expects that.

13
00:00:51,651 --> 00:00:55,356
Break the mapping and tools get angry fast.

14
00:00:55,356 --> 00:00:59,406
Folder path and package declaration must agree.

15
00:00:59,406 --> 00:01:03,096
Packages define package-private visibility.

16
00:01:03,096 --> 00:01:07,388
Types in the same package can collaborate quietly.

17
00:01:07,388 --> 00:01:12,537
Types outside must use the public API — if you designed one.

18
00:01:12,537 --> 00:01:17,515
Good packages make invalid dependencies hard to introduce.

19
00:01:17,515 --> 00:01:21,806
Bad packages — one giant folder — erase ownership.

20
00:01:21,806 --> 00:01:25,840
Boundaries only work if the tree reflects them.

21
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,852
Organize by capability when you can.

22
00:01:28,852 --> 00:01:32,282
api. application. domain. infrastructure.

23
00:01:32,282 --> 00:01:38,054
Or by feature — orders, payments, shipping — when teams own features.

24
00:01:38,054 --> 00:01:42,404
Layered-only packages can become anemic and tangled.

25
00:01:42,404 --> 00:01:46,921
Pick a structure that mirrors how people own the code.

26
00:01:46,921 --> 00:01:52,610
Feature teams and domain packages often fit better than pure layers.

27
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:58,596
Spring Boot tip — scanning starts from the main class package downward.

28
00:01:58,596 --> 00:02:02,053
Put OrdersApplication at a sensible root.

29
00:02:02,053 --> 00:02:06,269
Bury it too deep and beans disappear mysteriously.

30
00:02:06,269 --> 00:02:11,497
Packages are not only organization — frameworks navigate them.

31
00:02:11,497 --> 00:02:13,773
Design the root on purpose.

32
00:02:13,773 --> 00:02:17,652
A wrong root creates mysterious missing beans.

33
00:02:17,652 --> 00:02:19,509
Three common mistakes.

34
00:02:19,509 --> 00:02:24,152
One — every class in one package called util or common.

35
00:02:24,152 --> 00:02:29,892
Two — cyclic dependencies between packages — architecture spaghetti.

36
00:02:29,892 --> 00:02:35,633
Three — main class buried so component scanning misses half the app.

37
00:02:35,633 --> 00:02:39,431
Also — package names that lie about contents.

38
00:02:39,431 --> 00:02:43,990
Honest names reduce wrong imports and wrong ownership.

39
00:02:43,990 --> 00:02:47,689
Interview question — why do packages matter?

40
00:02:47,689 --> 00:02:53,659
Namespace uniqueness. Access boundaries. Ownership. Framework scanning.

41
00:02:53,659 --> 00:02:59,460
Runtime identity is package plus class name — plus classloader later.

42
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:03,664
Good structure makes illegal dependencies awkward.

43
00:03:03,664 --> 00:03:08,120
That is architecture you can feel in the folder tree.

44
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:14,226
Boundaries are set. Next — fixed sets of constants with behavior.

45
00:03:14,226 --> 00:03:16,574
Episode Thirteen — enums.

46
00:03:16,574 --> 00:03:20,519
Type-safe states instead of magic strings.

47
00:03:20,519 --> 00:03:21,834
See you there.
